WHY IT IS ONLY A MARKER
-----------------------
§16g's probe shape is IDENTICAL to §16f's: `C` is still at most
`foreclosure_conjunction_width` (3) literals to flip from a base that RECORDS
the member, and a member that gets recorded REFUTES the inference. So
`coverage.wellfounded_drops_to_dict()` emits the §16g classes as the SAME
`SubconjunctionForeclosureAssumption` type, with the SAME `conditioning` /
`gates` / `expr` / `prefix` payload, plus two new informational keys
(`wellfounded: True`, `section: "16g"`).

CONSEQUENCE: the gate ALREADY TESTS §16g CLASSES CORRECTLY WITH NO EDIT.
  * `test_declared` dispatches on `type` -> `_test_subconjunction_foreclosure`
    -> `_test_conjunction_foreclosure(..., kind="subconj")`   (line 2295-2296)
  * the verdict-row dedup key already carries `prefix` (line 2614), so a §16f
    and a §16g claim over the SAME (C, member) collapse to one row — which is
    right: identical claim, identical probe, one replay.
  * the extra keys are ignored by every consumer.

The staged diff below therefore changes NOTHING about which probes run or what
verdict they get. It only carries the provenance into the verdict row so a
report can say how many PASS/FAIL rows belong to §16f and how many to §16g.

